### 1. Task outcome (short version):
Extracted structured evidence from 9 included studies on cotrimoxazole prophylaxis in HIV-infected children and 
HIV-exposed uninfected infants. Across CHAP (Zambia), cotrimoxazole reduced mortality and hospitalizations (mainly 
respiratory infections), improved growth/hemoglobin, and post-trial benefits persisted; ART availability further reduced
events. In HEU infants (Botswana), CTX did not improve growth but increased commensal CTX resistance. In Uganda (high 
malaria transmission), daily TMP-SMX reduced malaria incidence by ~49% vs no chemoprevention (RR≈0.51).

### 2. Task outcome (extremely detailed version):
- CHAP RCT (Zambia): Cotrimoxazole vs placebo lowered mortality (notably out-of-hospital and post-admission; P=0.01 and 
0.005), reduced serious bacterial infection admissions (rate 19.1 vs 28.5/100 CY; RR≈0.67; P=0.09), and reduced 
in-hospital deaths from serious bacterial infections (2-yr 7% vs 12%; P=0.08). Trial-period mortality and 
hospitalization rate ratios vs placebo ≈0.61 and ≈0.69, respectively. Post-trial initiation of CTX in prior placebo 
recipients reduced rates; ART availability dropped mortality to 6 then 2 per 100 CY and admissions to 17 then 8 per 100 
CY. Prognostic analyses identified malnutrition and prior respiratory/bacterial hospitalizations as independent 
mortality predictors.
- Resistance/carriage (Zambia CHAP substudy): CTX increased CTX resistance in S. pneumoniae (P<0.0001) and H. influenzae
(P=0.005) and altered switching dynamics toward resistance; carriage patterns minimally affected overall.
- Growth/hematology (CHAP): CTX slowed declines in WAZ (P=0.04) and HAZ (P=0.01) and increased hemoglobin (P=0.01).
- Adherence (CHAP): Every 10% lower bottle-weight adherence associated with ~10–11% higher mortality risk 
(HR≈1.10–1.11); caregiver-reported adherence not predictive.
- Botswana Mpepu (HEU infants): Prolonged infant CTX (to 15 months) did not impact mean WAZ/LAZ/WLZ through 18 months; 
substudy showed markedly higher CTX-resistant E. coli and Klebsiella at 3 and 6 months among CTX recipients.
- Uganda Tororo (HEU, high malaria): Daily TMP-SMX reduced malaria incidence by 49% (RR≈0.51; 95% CI 0.34–0.77; P=0.001)
vs no chemoprevention; monthly DP had greater efficacy; secondary outcomes largely similar across arms; regimens well 
tolerated.

### 3. Additional context (if relevant):
ART context: Most CHAP participants were ART-naive during the trial; later ART availability yielded large additional 
reductions in mortality/hospitalizations. Malaria endemicity: Strong TMP-SMX benefit observed in a high-transmission 
Ugandan setting. Dosing details: CHAP used once-daily CTX; detailed mg/kg dosing not specified in abstracts. Infant 
trials initiated CTX in early infancy (Botswana 14–34 days to 15 months). Antimicrobial resistance: CTX prophylaxis 
selects for CTX-resistant commensals; clinical implications need longer-term evaluation.
