# Narrative synthesis                                                                                                   
synthesis_text = (                                                                                                      
    "Primary outcomes — mortality and serious infections:\n"                                                            
    "- HIV-infected children (CHAP, Zambia): Two randomized, placebo-controlled publications from the CHAP trial        
consistently show that daily cotrimoxazole reduces mortality and serious infection–related morbidity compared with      
placebo. During the blinded trial phase, estimated rate ratios favored cotrimoxazole for all-cause mortality (RR≈0.61)  
and hospital admissions (RR≈0.69) [4]; admissions for serious bacterial infections were lower (19.1 vs 28.5 per 100     
child-years; RR≈0.67; P=0.09) and in-hospital death from serious bacterial infection was numerically reduced (7% vs 12% 
at 2 years; P=0.08) [3]. Additional analyses reported significantly lower mortality outside hospital (P=0.01) and       
following hospital admission (P=0.005) with cotrimoxazole [3]. After the trial ended, initiation of cotrimoxazole in    
prior placebo recipients was associated with declines in deaths and admissions, and the later availability of ART was   
associated with further large reductions in both outcomes [4]. Pneumocystis pneumonia (PCP)–specific events were not    
reported in these abstracts; benefits appeared concentrated in respiratory infections overall [3].\n"                   
    "\n"                                                                                                                
    "Secondary outcomes:\n"                                                                                             
    "- Hospitalizations: Consistently reduced during the trial with cotrimoxazole vs placebo (RR≈0.69) [4]; serious     
bacterial infection admissions trend lower (RR≈0.67) [3].\n"                                                            
    "- Malaria (HEU children in malaria-endemic Uganda): In an open-label RCT, daily trimethoprim–sulfamethoxazole      
reduced malaria incidence vs no chemoprevention (RR 0.51, 95% CI 0.34–0.77) [13]. Monthly dihydroartemisinin–piperaquine
performed better, but TMP-SMX still conferred substantial protection [13].\n"                                           
    "- Growth and hematologic outcomes: Among HIV-infected children, cotrimoxazole was associated with slower declines  
in weight-for-age and height-for-age and greater hemoglobin increases [6]. In contrast, among HIV-exposed uninfected    
(HEU) infants in Botswana, prolonged prophylaxis to 15 months showed no effect on growth through 18 months [11].\n"     
    "- Antimicrobial resistance (AMR): In HIV-infected children in Zambia, cotrimoxazole prophylaxis increased          
resistance to cotrimoxazole among nasopharyngeal Streptococcus pneumoniae and Haemophilus influenzae and altered        
transition probabilities toward resistance [5]. In HEU infants in Botswana, cotrimoxazole prophylaxis markedly increased
cotrimoxazole resistance among commensal E. coli and Klebsiella spp. at 3 and 6 months [14]. Across settings, CTX       
prophylaxis consistently selected for CTX-resistant commensals/pathogens.\n"                                            
    "- Adverse events: None of the included abstracts reported clinical adverse events in detail; safety signals were   
not highlighted in these summaries [3][4][5][6][11][13][14].\n"                                                         
    "\n"                                                                                                                
    "Heterogeneity and context:\n"                                                                                      
    "- Population: Strong mortality and hospitalization benefits are demonstrated in HIV-infected, ART-naive African    
children [3][4], whereas benefits in HEU infants are context-dependent: growth-neutral in Botswana [11] but             
malaria-reducing in high-transmission Uganda [13].\n"                                                                   
    "- Setting and endemicity: Malaria protection with TMP-SMX is evident in a setting with intense transmission [13],  
which may not generalize to low-transmission areas.\n"                                                                  
    "- ART context: In CHAP, most children were ART-naive during the trial; later ART roll-out further reduced mortality
and admissions [4]. This suggests cotrimoxazole benefits persist pre-ART and that combined ART+CTX may yield additive   
reductions, though post-trial observations are non-randomized.\n"                                                       
    "- Antimicrobial resistance background: High baseline resistance was noted in Zambia and Botswana, yet clinical     
benefits on mortality/hospitalization persisted in HIV-infected children [3][4][6], underscoring that cotrimoxazole’s   
net clinical effect may extend beyond direct activity against fully susceptible organisms, potentially via prevention of
some bacterial/respiratory infections.\n"                                                                               
    "\n"                                                                                                                
    "Certainty and implications:\n"                                                                                     
    "- Mortality/hospitalizations in HIV-infected children: Moderate-to-high certainty based on a large, blinded RCT    
program in one country with consistent effects and biologic plausibility [3][4].\n"                                     
    "- Malaria in HEU infants: Moderate certainty for protection in malaria-endemic settings from one open-label RCT    
[13].\n"                                                                                                                
    "- Growth outcomes: Low-to-moderate certainty—signals of improved growth/hemoglobin in HIV-infected children [6],   
but no growth effect in HEU infants [11]; these outcomes are secondary to the main clinical endpoints.\n"               
    "- AMR selection: Moderate certainty that CTX prophylaxis increases CTX resistance among commensal/pathogenic       
bacteria in both HIV-infected children and HEU infants [5][14]; clinical consequences were not quantified.\n"           
    "Overall, routinely administered cotrimoxazole reduces mortality and serious illness (notably respiratory           
infections) in HIV-infected African children and reduces malaria in HEU children in high-transmission settings, while   
selecting for CTX resistance in carriage flora. Adverse events were underreported in the included abstracts. Practice   
should continue to prioritize CTX in HIV-infected children (including with ART), with local malaria epidemiology guiding
CTX use in HEU infants; surveillance for AMR and careful stewardship remain important. Evidence gaps include            
PCP-specific outcomes, adverse event quantification, multicountry trials in the ART era, and the clinical impact of AMR 
selection."                                                                                                             
