Final answer: ### 1. Task outcome (short version):
Cotrimoxazole prophylaxis reduces mortality and hospitalizations in HIV-infected children in Zambia’s CHAP RCT 
(mortality RR≈0.61; hospitalizations RR≈0.69) and lowers serious respiratory infection–related deaths [3][4]. In 
HIV-exposed uninfected infants, effects vary by context: no growth benefit in Botswana [11], but malaria incidence 
reduced by ~49% in high-transmission Uganda [13]. Across settings, cotrimoxazole selects for CTX-resistant 
commensal/pathogenic bacteria [5][14]. Safety was underreported. Overall evidence strength: moderate.

### 2. Task outcome (extremely detailed version):
Quality and bias: High-quality RCT evidence from CHAP ([3][4]) demonstrates consistent reductions in mortality and 
serious illness with daily cotrimoxazole versus placebo among ART-naive HIV-infected children, with post-trial 
observational data suggesting persistence of benefits and additional reductions following ART roll-out. Secondary 
analyses indicate improved growth and hemoglobin [6]. In HEU infants, a large Botswana RCT found no growth benefit [11],
while an open-label Ugandan RCT showed significant malaria prevention (RR 0.51) [13]. Two randomized substudies showed 
robust selection for cotrimoxazole resistance in S. pneumoniae, H. influenzae, E. coli, and Klebsiella spp. [5][14]. Key
biases/limitations include: reliance on one country’s RCT program for mortality/hospitalization endpoints; lack of 
PCP-specific data; minimal adverse event reporting; open-label design for the malaria trial; and observational 
post-trial periods confounded by secular trends. Implications: Continue cotrimoxazole for HIV-infected children 
(including with ART), consider CTX for HEU infants in high malaria transmission settings, and strengthen AMR 
surveillance. Research needs: multicountry ART-era RCTs with standardized mortality, PCP, hospitalization, safety, and 
AMR endpoints; evaluations of the clinical significance of AMR selection.

### 3. Additional context (if relevant):
ART context matters: CHAP trial effects predated ART access; combined ART+CTX appeared additive in observational 
follow-up. Endemicity drives malaria benefits in HEU infants; alternative regimens (e.g., 
dihydroartemisinin–piperaquine) may outperform CTX where feasible [13]. Despite high background resistance, net clinical
benefits on mortality/hospitalizations were observed in HIV-infected children, suggesting complex mechanisms or partial 
activity.

